(project tuna-accounts
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: the pp-slice - tuna as a pricklypear-like live habitat slice. Two credential tiers over ONE identity store (bearer tokens for agents, opaque cookie sessions for browsers), admin-only identity minting with non-admin defaults, and the anonymous public rim (welcome, /code, /agent.txt, /.well-known/agent.json, /src.tgz). Research question, stated so it can be refuted: a tree-calculus habitat whose access law is already 'the route record is the capability' (borg/routes.borg law 3) needs NO second permission matrix for a human tier - browser sessions can ride the same identities rows and the same path-prefix grants, so the whole pp-slice lands as additive credential tables plus reserved public surfaces, never a new authz model.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section accounts
  (doc "The identity store stays singular: `identities` (0001) is the one subject table; the pp-slice adds credentials, sessions, and an audit log beside it, and the public face is reserved surfaces that match before the route dispatcher (routes.borg law 2). Nothing here resolves names at a prim boundary - grants remain arguments (AGENTS 7).")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection credential-tiers
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Tuna has ONE subject table (`identities`, migration 0001;
      `is_admin` gates only the four admin acts - mint identities,
      route publish, grant admin view, others' grants) and now two
      credential tiers over it, both resolving to a `Store.identity`:

      BEARER (agent tier, unchanged).  `Authorization: Bearer
      <token>` on every `/api/*` route; sha256 stored in
      `identities.token_hash`; boot mints root (TUNA_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN,
      printed once) and fed peers per TUNA_FED_PEERS.

      SESSION (human tier, the pp-slice).  Migration 0013 adds
      `credentials(kind, secret_hash, unique(identity_id, kind))`,
      `auth_sessions(token_hash, expires_at, revoked_at)` and
      `auth_log`.  POST /login accepts username+password OR a bearer
      token; BOTH mint an opaque session row (32 random bytes hex;
      only its sha256 is stored; 14-day TTL) and set the
      `tuna_session` cookie HttpOnly + SameSite=Lax.  The cookie
      carries ONLY the session token - it supersedes the v0 stance
      where the cookie carried a raw bearer.  Logout revokes the
      row.  `Api.authenticate` keeps bearer FIRST (agents) and falls
      back to the session cookie (browser clients on /api/*).

      INTERIM HASHING, NAMED.  `credentials.secret_hash` uses the
      PP-compatible interim string `sha256$salt$digest` with
      `digest = sha256(salt_hex ^ ":" ^ password)`, salt from
      /dev/urandom.  Not a memory-hard KDF; argon2id is the planned
      drop-in and the format is string-compatible so a future
      upgrade verifies legacy rows and transparently rehashes on
      success - no flag day.  Verify is constant-time; an unknown
      username is timing-flattened against a fixed dummy hash so the
      endpoint does not oracle account existence.  No CSRF token and
      no login throttle yet; `auth_log` exists so a throttle/forensics
      pass has data instead of guesses.

      BOOT.  `TUNA_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` (optional) ensures root's
      password credential each boot; setting a new value rotates it.
      Unset means no insecure default - root signs in by its bearer
      token at the login page's token form. |))))
  (subsection identity-minting
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Identity minting is the ONE admin gate that is not a grant
      (the subject exists before any grant can reference it):
      `POST /api/identities` and the `/identities` htmx page mint a
      NON-ADMIN identity by default (`admin=true` is an explicit
      opt-in) and return its bearer token ONCE alongside an optional
      initial password.  `GET /api/identities` is the roster, no
      token hashes.  A non-admin gets 403 on mint.

      BOUNDARY AUDIT (the plan's T2 question).  A non-admin identity
      already behaves as intended because the authority is the grant
      row, not the identity kind: it can run/compile, keep its own
      REPL dictionary (per-identity dest), mint/attenuate/revoke its
      OWN grants (store holder checks), and read public pages.  It
      cannot mint identities (new is_admin gate), revoke others'
      grants (holder check), or write outside its path-prefix garden
      (grants are the only capability at a prim boundary, and route
      publish requires a covering grant).  No second permission
      matrix was added - the audit found no hole that needed one. |))))
  (subsection public-rim
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      The hard-public face is reserved (routes.borg law 2 list grew
      by this book change: welcome, code, agent.txt, src.tgz,
      .well-known, identities), so a route record can never shadow
      it.  All anonymous, matching before the route dispatcher:

      /welcome   front page: what tuna is, the calculus in two
                 lines, links to /code, the manifests, /src.tgz,
                 /login, /health.
      /code      read-only browser for newest programs + runs
                 (data-first; a server page, not a route record,
                 because it carries no capability and lists dynamic
                 rows - recorded call per the routes chapter).
      /agent.txt / /.well-known/agent.json   agent manifest
                 generated in OCaml (server/lib/agent.ml, shaped
                 after PP's agent_discovery): auth model, first
                 calls, endpoint census, replay + deriv verify
                 surfaces, htmx hints.  Base URL derived from the
                 request's forwarded host headers, so it is correct
                 behind a reverse proxy.
      /src.tgz   the server's own source at HEAD.  Generated by
                 scripts/deploy/build-public.sh (`git archive HEAD`,
                 no _build, no secrets) into TUNA_PUBLIC_DIR and
                 served from disk by Dream.from_filesystem - no
                 github dependency at serve time.  Reflects HEAD at
                 the last build-public run, not necessarily the
                 running binary.
      /health    tokenless liveness, unchanged. |))))
  (subsection acceptance
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. PUBLIC SURFACES.  Against a live isolated instance,
         /welcome /code /agent.txt /.well-known/agent.json /health
         answer 200 anonymously; /src.tgz is a valid gzipped tar;
         unknown public paths are plain 404.  `scripts/smoke-public.sh`.
      2. SESSION TIER.  Password login mints a session cookie; the
         cookie drives /api/*; logout revokes it and the same cookie
         then 401s.  Bearer still works on /api/*; unauthenticated
         /api/* is 401.  `tests/session_tests.ml` (PG-gated, own
         scratch db) + smoke-public [10].
      3. MINTING.  An admin mints a NON-ADMIN identity + password;
         the returned bearer token authenticates; the minted identity
         is 403 on identity mint; the admin /identities page shows
         the form and a non-admin sees an explanation, not the form.
         smoke-public [8][9][11].
      4. BOOK/REALITY.  `borge lint && borge report` clean; the
         routes reserved list transcribed in server/lib/routes.ml
         matches the pinned list in borg/routes.borg.

      OUTCOMES 2026-10-02 - green.
      - T1 (0013) + tests/session_tests.ml green under scratch db.
      - T2 API + /identities page; smoke-public [8][9] green.
      - T3 public face (agent.ml + pages/public.ml + src.tgz slot);
        smoke-public [1]-[6][12] green.
      - T4 scripts/serve.sh + scripts/deploy/{build-public.sh,
        tuna.service,reverse-proxy.md}; serve.sh start boots PG +
        migrations + build + src.tgz + server on :18092.
      - T5 this chapter + only one inline line in tuna.borg; full
        `dune build @all && dune runtest tests` green, all three
        smokes (public + api + ui) green against :18092. |))))
  (subsection pre-registered-failures
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      F10 SESSION CONFUSION.  The two credential tiers drift into
      two authorization models (a session that is implicitly more
      privileged than its bearer, or a second permission matrix
      beside the grant rows).  TRIGGER: any pp-slice change that
      resolves a session to anything other than its identity's
      grants + is_admin.  THEN: it is a book issue - the authority
      is the grant row (AGENTS 7); revert, or amend this chapter
      and routes.borg together in the open.

      F11 RIM SHADOWING.  A route record begins serving a reserved
      public path (a stale reserved list, a new /src-like surface
      added in code without the book change).  TRIGGER: dispatch
      answers a reserved site path, or the transcribed list in
      routes.ml diverges from borg/routes.borg.  THEN: the book
      change lands FIRST (the list grows by book change, never in
      code), the code list follows in the same commit, and a
      regression test pins the collision denial. |)))))
)
